    "q-275157": {
      "dom_id": "step-q275157",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-4+6\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226152/assets/325444dbc509efd27f6c5bf16c751b137532e2566a9dfaa69f92d9ce16bac51b.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{6}{4}) \\\\  & =0.982\\text{ }793…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi -0.982\\text{ }793…≈2.16,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
