    "q-8257": {
      "dom_id": "step-q8257",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-3+4\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226152/assets/373ea8254a9816f67bfbcaf993348f409c1047284d09c43e5d41e68b15c5af58.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\pi .$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.927\\,30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈\\pi -0.927\\,30≈2.21\n$$\n\nto two decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
