    "q-319509": {
      "dom_id": "step-q319509",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-7-3\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226152/assets/1d131288e3d38767e1d82b68777e69667711e98665101093f3b7e544e9caea1e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan\\,(\\frac{3}{7})=0.404\\,892…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi +0.404\\,892…≈3.55,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
