    "q-8452": {
      "dom_id": "step-q8452",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-5-3\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226152/assets/9596107dc58d6acb67f1014ebc17e632dd03173aa975fa84b196588db873d8e5.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})≈0.540\\,42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈\\pi +0.540\\,42≈3.68\n$$\n\nto two decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
